git init - (creates repo)
t creates a Local Repository, but it does not create a Remote Repository.

In Git terminology, a "repository" (repo for short) simply means a folder where Git is actively tracking your files, history, and changes.

Here is the difference:

What git init does (Local): It creates a private repository right on your hard drive. It exists entirely on your computer, and only you can see it.

What GitHub does (Remote): When you click "New Repository" on GitHub's website, you are creating a remote repository 
on the internet that can be shared and accessed from anywhere.

So, when you type git init in your VS Code terminal, you have successfully created a repo! It is just an isolated one that isn't connected to the cloud yet.
 
