*****Branch**********
Think of Git like a video game where you can save your progress, and branches as alternate timelines.

Imagine you have a website that is live and working perfectly on the main branch. You want to build a risky new "Dark Mode" feature, but it will take you three days, and the code will be broken while you write it.

If you work directly on main, your live website code becomes broken.

Instead, you create a new branch called dark-mode.

Git essentially splits your timeline.

You can now write code, break things, and make commits on the dark-mode branch.

Meanwhile, your main branch remains completely safe, untouched, and working.

-----git branch command shows the available branches---------
To create a new branch and switch to it : git switch -c (here c means create . create a new branch and immediately jump to it)
To only switch to another branch , just write git switch
